.page kill 1 send a signal to processes

# SYNOPSIS
*kill* [*-*_SIGNAL_ | *-s* _SIGNAL_] _PID_...
*kill* *-l*

# DESCRIPTION
`kill` sends a signal (SIGTERM by default) to each process _PID_. A _SIGNAL_ is a number or a name with or without the `SIG` prefix: `kill -9 12`, `kill -KILL 12`, `kill -s SIGUSR1 12`. A process may catch most signals; `KILL` (9) and `STOP` cannot be caught, which is why the login profile defines the alias `exorcise` as `kill -9`.

The signals known by name are HUP INT QUIT ILL TRAP ABRT BUS FPE KILL USR1 SEGV USR2 PIPE ALRM TERM CHLD CONT STOP TSTP TTIN TTOU.

`kill -STOP` freezes a process (all its threads): `ps` shows state `T` until `kill -CONT` lets it run again. `TSTP` is the stop that Ctrl-Z sends; a program may catch or ignore it, `STOP` it cannot. `TTIN` and `TTOU` stop a background job that wanted the terminal. `CONT` always continues a stopped process, even one that blocks or ignores it.

gapsh has a builtin `kill` as well. It does the same and also accepts job specifications such as `%1`.

# OPTIONS
@ *-l*
    List the signal names with their numbers.
@ *-s* _SIGNAL_, *-*_SIGNAL_
    The signal to send.

# EXIT STATUS
0 if every signal was sent, 1 if a process did not exist, the signal was unknown or the permission was refused; the message names the process.

# EXAMPLES
    kill 123
    kill -9 123 124
    kill -s INT 77
    kill -l

# SEE ALSO
ps(1), pkill(1), pidof(1), gapsh(1)
